For a conditional equation the graphs of $y = LHS$ and $y = RHS$ **intersect at one point**.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\small}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
\draw[<->] (-3.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-3.4) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->] (-2.17,-3) -- (2.83,3);
\draw[thick,red!75!black,dashed,<->] (-0.5,3) -- (3,0.2);
\fill (1.5,1.4) circle (2.2pt);
\node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (1.5,2.05) {$P$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For an identity the two graphs are **coincident** lines — the same gradient and the same $y$-intercept.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\small}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
\draw[<->] (-3.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-3.4) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->] (-3,-2.4) -- (3,3);
\draw[thick,red!75!black,dash pattern=on 9pt off 9pt,<->] (-3,-2.4) -- (3,3);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For a contradiction the two graphs are **parallel** lines — the same gradient but a different $y$-intercept.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\small}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
\draw[<->] (-3.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-3.4) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->] (-3,-1.5) -- (2,3);
\draw[thick,red!75!black,dashed,<->] (-2,-3) -- (3,1.5);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
a. Both sides give $y=x+1$. The graphs have the same gradient and the same $y$-intercept, so they are coincident. Every real value of $x$ satisfies the equation: it is an identity.

The solid blue line and dashed red line lie exactly on top of each other.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{seriesblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{seriesred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[step=7mm,gridgrey,very thin] (-3,-3) grid (3,4);
\draw[<->,black] (-3.3,0)--(3.3,0);
\draw[<->,black] (0,-3.3)--(0,4.3);
\path (3.3,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,4.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {
\draw[black] (\v,0.06)--(\v,-0.06);
\path (\v,-0.06) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};
}
\foreach \v in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4} {
\draw[black] (0.06,\v)--(-0.06,\v);
\path (-0.06,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};
}
\path (-0.08,-0.08) node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$0$};
\draw[seriesblue,very thick,<->] (-3,-2)--(3,4);
\draw[seriesred,thick,dashed] (-3,-2)--(3,4);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

b. The graphs are $y=x+1$ and $y=x-2$. Both have gradient $1$, but their $y$-intercepts are $1$ and $-2$. They are parallel and never meet, so no value of $x$ satisfies the equation: it is a contradiction.

The blue line represents $y=x+1$; the dashed red line represents $y=x-2$.

